  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nNow-Tonight...A healthy field of cumulus clouds is present across \neast central Florida this afternoon, now highlighting the \ndeveloping east coast sea breeze near I-95. Showers are forming near \nand west of the breeze, in an environment characterized by 2.2\"+ PW \n(see 15z XMR sounding), 2,000-2,500 J/kg MLCAPE, weak lapse rates, \nand warm H5 temperatures. Like yesterday, not every location will \nsee rain or storms this afternoon. However, the spots that do may \nexperience torrential downpours, occasional to frequent lightning, \nand gusty winds. While initial convection will be focused closer to \nthe coast through 3 PM or so, a transition of rain/storms inland is \nexpected as both the sea breeze and 500mb flow shifts more onshore. \nCAMs and HREF alike push most of the activity west of the area by 8-\n9 PM as it all begins to dissipate past sunset. \n\nTonight, temperatures will settle into the 70s again but stay a few \ndegrees warmer at the coast. This marks the beginning of deepening \nonshore flow and the influence of an expanding surface ridge to our \nnorth. Showers and isolated storms are supported in both short and \nmedium-range guidance, and some of this activity could drift onshore \nafter midnight (closer to daybreak Tuesday). \n\nTuesday-Thursday...Some showers and a few storms over the local \nAtlantic may be moving onshore at the immediate coast Tuesday \nmorning. East-northeasterly winds become established areawide, \nincreasing a bit through the day, especially along the coast. This \nis in response to a gradually tightening pressure gradient around \nthe southern periphery of a ridge, centered over New England. A 10-\n15 mph breeze Tuesday becomes 15-20+ on Wednesday with peak gusts \naround 25-30 mph at times. Beach and boating conditions will become \npoor to hazardous, especially on Wednesday. As always, heed \nlifeguard advice, beach flags, and signs. Entering the surf is not \nadvised as conditions worsen mid week. \n\nLower rain chances continue to be advertised on Wednesday as a batch \nof drier air moves across north-central Florida. Despite model \nsoundings highlighting a stout dry layer between 500-700mb, the low \nlevels may remain moist enough to support shallow marine-layer \nshowers that repeatedly push toward the coast. Lower chances for \nlightning (generally less than 30-40%) are forecast Wednesday. High \nPW values return Thursday afternoon and beyond, coincident with an \nupper low passing overhead. With this additional dynamic support, \nrain chances return to 60% and higher Thursday. Guidance is also \nhighlighting some notable DCAPE values and H5 temps between -8C and -\n10C, so the potential for a few stronger storms and locally heavy \nrainfall will be monitored. \n\nFriday-Sunday (previous)...The mid to upper level ridge over the \nsoutheast U.S. gradually breaks down late week as troughing aloft \ncontinues to push west of Florida and into the Gulf. Southwest to \nsouth flow behind the trough aloft will advect a column of deeper \nmoisture over central Florida. This looks to support continued rain \nchances areawide into the weekend. High temperatures are forecast \nnear seasonal values mostly in the upper 80s and near 90 degrees.",
